development.solution_text:
Looking down, the outline is a rectangle as wide as the widest part of the prism.
The flat top face meets each sloping face along an edge, so two lines are drawn inside the outline.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.7]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (3,3.4);
\draw (0.8,0)--(0.8,3.4);
\draw (2.2,0)--(2.2,3.4);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

From the front you look straight at the five-sided end face.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.7]
\draw (0,0)--(3,0)--(3,1.3)--(1.5,2.6)--(0,1.3)--cycle;
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

From the front you look straight at the end of both prisms at once, which together make an L-shape.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.7]
\draw (0,0)--(3.6,0)--(3.6,1.2)--(1.5,1.2)--(1.5,2.8)--(0,2.8)--cycle;
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Looking down, the outline is one rectangle covering both prisms.
The top of the taller prism and the top of the shorter prism are at different heights, so a line is drawn where the surface drops.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.7]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (3.2,2.8);
\draw (1.3,0)--(1.3,2.8);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

From the side the outline is a rectangle as wide as the solid is long and as tall as the taller prism.
The top of the shorter prism runs the whole length of the solid at a lower height, so a line is drawn across the rectangle.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.7]
\draw (0,0) rectangle (3,2.6);
\draw (0,1.4)--(3,1.4);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

From the front you look straight at the ends of all three prisms at once, which together make a step shape.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.7]
\draw (0,0)--(3.6,0)--(3.6,0.9)--(2.4,0.9)--(2.4,1.8)--(1.2,1.8)--(1.2,2.7)--(0,2.7)--cycle;
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

